[{:seon.task/name "docs-are-a-wiki"
  :seon.task/plan "next/docs/ becomes a wiki that is always accurate (owner, 2026-10-21: \"a wiki style set of docs … always accurate and up to date and we can reference in the docstrings and task plans to pull in relevant details\"). One concept per page, docs/<topic>.md: system (root's procs, one paragraph each), agents (fork, branch, run-ahead writer, nREPL), tasks (lifecycle, task files, refinement, stuck, quiescence), merge (three-way keys, declared data, behind, unmerged, uncontracted, trains), store (Datahike on LMDB, branches, commits, kept runs, branch from any commit), program (definitions from the compiler, scratch namespaces, what judges), printer (seon.print and :seon.render/ai, :seon.render/html; no clipping), cost (seon.cost/proof, units, seon.profile), door (bin/root, bin/mcp, the tools), worktrees (bin/worktree, landing), check (every bin/check mode and what it proves). docs/README.md is the index. A section is addressed as \"topic#heading-slug\" (the heading's GitHub slug). Each section ends with a line naming the code it describes, as EDN: `Describes: {:vars [seon.merge/program] :attrs [:seon.root/quiescent] :files [\"src/seon/merge.clj\"]}`. Seed from what is true today: vocabulary.md, forks.md and DRAGONS.md fold in; notes/audit-2026-10-21/PROCESSES-draft.md and MAP-draft.md are the raw material for system and agents. Every claim is checked against the code as you write it (read the code, eval at the door); no page repeats another (link instead)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/every-page-is-indexed seon.docs-test/every-section-describes-something}}
 {:seon.task/name "docs-references-are-data"
  :seon.task/plan "A reference to the wiki is data, never prose to be parsed: a definition carries {:seon/docs [\"merge#behind\"]} in its metadata (recorded with the var, as :var/meta already is), a task carries :seon.task/docs #{\"merge#behind\"} (add it to seon.task/declaration and seon.task/own in resources/seon/schemas/seon.task.edn; task files may name it). seon.docs/section answers one section's text from its address (the markdown read by headings: a heading line starts a section, its slug is its address). Docstrings and task plans name docs by these keys; existing plans that cite notes/ or line numbers for an explanation get a docs reference instead as they are touched."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-are-a-wiki"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/a-reference-resolves-to-its-section seon.task-test/a-task-file-declares-docs}}
 {:seon.task/name "context-pulls-referenced-docs"
  :seon.task/plan "The agent's context includes the sections its task names (:seon.task/docs) and the sections named by the definitions the task's proofs call (their :seon/docs), each once (context says each thing once), through the context's own render (a section is a value with a :seon.render/ai; never clipped: a long section is elided by the printer with how to read the rest, e.g. (doc-section \"merge#behind\")). The door's seon_context shows the same. Recorded per turn like every context part (its characters counted in the per-turn context split)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-references-are-data"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-task-context-includes-its-docs}}
 {:seon.task/name "docs-stay-accurate"
  :seon.task/plan "bin/check docs (a seon.check mode, ends on DOCS OK / DOCS FAILED): (1) every :seon/docs and :seon.task/docs reference resolves to a section; (2) every var a Describes line names resolves in the release binary (find-var after loading the program), every attr is in the schema, every file exists; (3) staleness, exact per definition: a section is stale when a var it names has a different source now than at the page's last commit (read both with the reader: the form defining it in `git show <page-commit>:<file>` against today's recorded :var/src; no line diffs, no regex), or an attr it names changed its schema entry; a stale section is a problem (:seon.problems class :docs) and the check lists them. Stale is red: a change that makes a section wrong updates the section in the same commit. Add the step to bin/check."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-are-a-wiki" "seon.docs/docs-references-are-data"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/a-dangling-reference-is-red seon.docs-test/a-missing-var-is-red seon.docs-test/a-section-older-than-its-code-is-stale}}]
